{
  "dom_id": "step-q141250",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P\\cdot Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{2}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{2}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4+2}={x}^{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $6.$",
